@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Services}/{GlobalValues.Service_Path_Part_Live_Region}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Service_Name_Live_Region @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Service_Name_Live_Region @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>

<p>
    Whilst making the Live Region Service available as a general service for developers could be open to misuse, I believe that the complexities and nuances involved in making 
    announcements via ARIA live regions mean that more good will come from providing this service than potential harm.
</p>
<p>
    Extensive testing was performed using differing pairings of screen readers and browsers, until the optimal timing settings were found, that allowed the service to make announcements
    in a consistent manor across the pairings.
</p>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>

<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <b>macOS/iPhone</b> paired with <b>Safari.</b> When tested via the Busy Indicator component. The first of two messages got clipped when there was only a 1 second delay between the messages.
        All other screen reader browser pairing that I tested handled this short delay, not that I would advise using such a short delay between messages.
        <p>
            <strong>Note:</strong> I currently have no plans on addressing this as its more of an edge case and the messages can be read in full from using the announcement history dialog.
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="flex-content justify-content-between">
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Left_Arrow_Modifier" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Services}/{GlobalValues.Service_Path_Part_Live_Region}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Installation}")">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Service_Name_Live_Region - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Right_Arrow_Modifier" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Services}/{GlobalValues.Service_Path_Part_Live_Region}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Api}")">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Service_Name_Live_Region - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
</div>
